package std.task;

/**
 * A computation that finishes later.
 *
 * Calling an `async` method returns a `Future` of its result without
 * running any of the method. The method runs as the future is polled, and
 * stops at each `await` whose future isn't ready yet:
 *
 * ```
 * async int total(&List<String> words) {
 *     int sum = 0;
 *     for (String word : words) {
 *         sum += await lengthOf(word);
 *     }
 *     return sum;
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * The standard library defines what a future is, not how futures are run.
 * An executor, which comes from a library, polls them: it calls `poll` and,
 * when the result is `Poll.Pending`, waits until the future's `Waker` is
 * woken before polling it again.
 *
 * # Borrowing
 *
 * The future of an `async` method holds its arguments, so it borrows
 * whatever they borrow, for as long as the future exists:
 *
 * ```
 * String text = "hello";
 * Future<int> future = lengthOf(&text);
 * text = "bye"; // error: `text` is borrowed by `future`
 * ```
 *
 * # Exceptions
 *
 * Calling an `async` method doesn't throw. Its exceptions come out of
 * `poll`, and so out of the `await` of its future.
 *
 * # Cancelling
 *
 * Dropping a future that isn't finished cancels it: it is never polled
 * again, and the values it holds are dropped.
 *
 * # Implementing `Future`
 *
 * `poll` makes as much progress as it can without blocking. It returns
 * `Poll.Ready` with the value once there is one, and `Poll.Pending`
 * otherwise. Before returning `Poll.Pending`, it arranges for
 * `cx.waker()` to be woken once polling again can make progress; a future
 * that doesn't is never polled again.
 */
interface Future<T> {
    /**
     * Makes progress without blocking, and returns the value if it is
     * ready. A future must not be polled again after it returned
     * `Poll.Ready`; the future of an `async` method throws an
     * `IllegalStateException` if it is.
     *
     * # Throws
     *
     * Whatever the computation throws.
     */
    mut Poll<T> poll(&mut Context cx) throws Exception;
}
